# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief
# The other half of defect 026's rule. A name in a function type is what a call
# made through a value of it is checked against, so it is written exactly where
# two parameters share a type. Here they do not: a `str` cannot reach the `i64`
# position or the other way round, so the positions cannot be confused and the
# names claim a check nobody is doing.
#
# This is the code a function type carrying a pointless name has earned since
# before the defect — what moved is where it is decided. The parser used to
# refuse the name on sight, which is a question about the SPELLING; only the
# lowering knows that two written types are one type, so the question moved to
# the checker with the rest of the rule.
#
# The fix stays `certain`: the positions cannot be confused, so dropping the
# name changes nothing a program can observe.
function relay(f: (function(from: str, count: i64) -> str)) -> str  #~ named_parameter_in_function_type named_parameter_in_function_type
    return f("one", 2)

function main()
    print("the type is the whole mistake, and nothing below it cascades")
